[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what counts as a change worth telling someone about. Two of these quietly pull in extra scope, so I'll mark
  them.

  The low-cost ones are edits to fields the task already has — assignment, status, due date. "Comment added" is
  different: it means building a comments feature, which is its own subsystem. "Any field edited" sounds generous but is
  how feeds become noise nobody reads — a typo fix in a description pinging six people teaches everyone to ignore the
  bell.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which task changes should generate a notification? → Assigned / reassigned, Status changed, Due date changed[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I put a design together: the stack. Nothing here constrains it except one [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, so this is
  an open choice — and it's the expensive one to change later.

  - [1mNode + SQLite, vanilla frontend[0m — one language across client and server, SQLite needs no separate process, and the
    existing [38;5;153mindex.html[39m stays the entry point. Fewest moving parts for a multi-user app that has to actually run.
  - [1mPython (FastAPI) + SQLite[0m — your global setup is Python-first (micromamba/uv, ruff, mypy already configured), so
    tooling and conventions are established. Costs a language boundary between server and browser.
  - [1mNode + React[0m — better if the task UI grows complex. A notification bell with an unread badge does not need a
    framework, and it adds a build step to a project that currently has none.

  [1mRecommendation: Node + SQLite with a vanilla frontend.[0m The interesting part of this project is the event/subscription
  model, and I'd rather that not compete for attention with a toolchain. That said, if you'd rather stay in Python where
  your linting and type-checking are already set up, that's a fair trade and I'd take it without argument.
